Hold the robot standing until the control stack drives the motors

The bridge applies `ctrl[i] = tau + kp*(q - q_meas) + kd*(dq - dq_meas)` straight from the
last received lowcmd. That message is default-constructed until one arrives, so every term
is zero and every motor is dead from the moment the simulator starts.

On the arm_sdk stack that was invisible, because the onboard-controller stand-in owned the
legs and lowcmd was never used. On the lowcmd stack it drops the robot. The simulator starts
stepping physics roughly six seconds before `G1LowCmdSystem` initialises and ten before any
controller claims a joint, so the robot is already prone by the time a balance policy exists.
The policy then gets a prone observation, diverges, and the safety controller latches its
out-of-domain emergency. `pin_pelvis` existed to paper over exactly this.

The hold has two parts, because a joint PD alone cannot balance a humanoid: measured, it
holds the joint angles perfectly well and the robot topples about its feet anyway, ending at
a pelvis height of 0.094 m. So the pose comes from a per-motor PD at the model's initial
configuration, and the balance comes from a weld the scene declares by name,
`pelvis_startup_hold`. Both are released together and never re-applied.

That is the parity-correct behaviour rather than a simulator convenience. On a real G1 the
onboard controller holds the robot standing until `MotionSwitcherClient::ReleaseMode()`
returns, and only then do the motors become ours. The simulator having no equivalent was the
gap.

Three details worth stating, each of which was a failed attempt first:

Handover triggers on a non-zero kp, kd or tau rather than on the arrival of a frame. The
hardware component publishes continuously from the moment it activates, and until a
controller claims a joint it sends that joint kp=kd=tau=0, so "a frame arrived" fires several
seconds before anything is actually holding the robot up.

The hold is tracked per motor rather than globally, so controllers spawning in any order hand
over one group at a time. Globally, the first controller to activate released every joint the
others had not claimed yet.

The weld is released only once every motor has been driven for half a second, so the robot is
never left balancing on a half-claimed body or on a policy that has not reached its stance.
Which controller activates last is a spawner race, so without that settle the weld sometimes
released on the very tick a balance policy first wrote, while its blend was still at zero and
its command was therefore a static pose. Measured, that dropped the robot about half the time. A scene that declares no such weld simply has nothing to release, which
is what keeps the arm_sdk scenes and the permanently-pinned test scene unchanged.

